description:
Jake Kennedy, who spent eight years working for Republicans on the Senate Environment and Public Works Committee, joined the lobbying and strategic communications firm CGCN Group as a partner on Monday.
Kennedy, who focused on chemical policy and regulation matters on Capitol Hill, worked for the panel’s former chairman, Sen. John Barrasso (R-Wyo.
), and most recently under Chairman Shelley Moore Capito (R-W.Va.).

text:
Colorado Gov. Jared Polis and the Colorado Energy Office (CEO) released the Colorado Geothermal Market Assessment report at the 2026 National Association of State Energy Officials (NASEO) last week.

The report evaluates the geothermal market, perceptions of geothermal technologies, and potential geothermal resources for heating/cooling and electricity generation. It also highlights the state’s leadership in geothermal. The report is designed to help state and local policymakers, utilities, project developers, investors, workforce development entities, and others to align on strategy to advance geothermal in Colorado.

“We know that by harnessing the heat beneath our feet we are saving Coloradans money. Colorado is showing the nation what is possible when you invest in innovative clean energy technologies to use natural resources to power, heat, and cool our homes. This new report is a roadmap to our geothermal future, backed by data driven strategies to streamline geothermal developments and increase access to geothermal energy across Colorado,” Polis said.

The assessment addresses:

Feasibility of different types of geothermal projects in each of the five regions;

Locations and project types of $42.6 million in State geothermal incentive awards, including 81% for thermal energy networks, 12% for geothermal electricity exploration, and 7% for direct use/waste heat recovery projects;

Impact of geothermal legislation from 2022 through 2026, including lessons learned to date;

Case studies of different geothermal technology applications;

Financing and business model exploration, especially for geothermal electricity;

Similarity in workforce skills and hiring demand between the geothermal, oil and gas, and other related sectors;

Barriers and benefits of geothermal adoption; and

Strategies to advance Colorado’s geothermal market potential.

“As Colorado makes rapid progress toward net-zero greenhouse gas pollution by 2050, geothermal technologies will play a huge role in keeping our homes warm, our lights on, and our air clean,” CEO Executive Director Will Toor said. “We are already seeing growing adoption of efficient geothermal heating and cooling in Colorado homes and businesses, alongside ongoing exploration into geothermal resources for clean, firm electricity generation and industrial uses.”

The report generated several key findings. It found that:

Geothermal for heating and cooling is a viable solution to reduce heating costs, support grid resilience, and improve air quality in all regions of Colorado.

For electricity generation, there are particularly strong near-term opportunities in the Denver-Julesburg Basin, where there is existing data about local geology from the oil and gas industry, and near retiring coal plants with existing electric transmission infrastructure.

The assessment also found a 60 percent overlap in geothermal workforce skills with the oil and gas sector, indicating geothermal’s potential to create jobs in communities most impacted by the transition away from fossil fuels.

CEO will showcase the Geothermal Market Assessment at a webinar on November 16 at 12:00 PM MT and at Colorado’s inaugural Geothermal Solutions Summit from Nov. 30 to Dec. 3 at the Colorado School of Mines.
